    <artifactId>surgery-service</artifactId>
    <name>Surgery — Surgery and Surgical Specialties domain pack</name>
    <description>
        Clinical-plane system of record for surgical DISEASE — a management-course record
        that attaches to PCT journeys and never contains them. Nothing owns this today.

        Per ADR-SURGERY-AND-PROCEDURES-SERVICE-BOUNDARIES decision 5, as corrected by the
        2026-07-26 CC-2 amendment (5a), this service MAY own: the surgical episode as a
        management-course record; structured surgical assessment content; operative
        indication and the non-operative options weighed; prehabilitation and optimisation
        execution; planned-versus-performed reconciliation; complication pathway INSTANCES
        as workflow (the resulting problem lands in pct_problems); waiting-list clinical
        revalidation state; and the fifteen specialty extensions.

        It MUST NOT own — these are PCT's, and surgery attaches to them, per care-continuum
        doctrine CC-2: surgical condition/diagnosis/certainty/severity/staging/recurrence
        (pct_problems, responsible_service=surgery); the surveillance plan (pct_care_plans
        + pct_care_plan_goals); the decision that opens or closes a phase of care (PCT
        decides, per the admission-handshake model; surgery owns the surgical REASONING and
        executes); functional/patient-reported outcomes (PCT person-level); serial burn
        assessment (pct.burn_assessment, emergency lane delegation).

        It is PCT-anchored per CC-5, references inpatient.procedure_episode for each
        operation, and consumes procedures-service for the readiness/competence/catalogue
        engine. It is NOT a second theatre workflow and must never create one.

        See docs/architecture/adr/ADR-SURGERY-AND-PROCEDURES-SERVICE-BOUNDARIES.md §5/§5a.
    </description>

